<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Peter Zenger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1697">1697</a>–<a href="/years/1746">1746</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Peter Zenger was a German-born printer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City whose New-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a> Weekly Journal attacked Governor William Cosby. Arrested for seditious libel, he was acquitted by a jury in 1735 after his lawyer Andrew Hamilton argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> the truth could not be libel. The case became a landmark in the history of press freedom in America.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-peter-zenger.jpg" alt="John Peter Zenger" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: [Andrew Hamilton defending John Peter Zenger in court, 1734-1735, unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Peter_Zenger_trial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zenger was born in 1697 in the German Palatinate, the son of Nicolaus Eberhard Zenger, a schoolteacher, and his wife Johanna. The family emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1710 with a large group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-palatines">German Palatines</a>; his father died before they settled. Under the governor's scheme for immigrants' children he was bound for eight years as an apprentice to William Bradford, the first printer in New York. He worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> by 1720, returned to New York by 1722, and in 1725 was responsible for producing Bradford's New York Gazette and briefly his partner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He then set up as a commercial printer on Smith Street, where in 1730 he published Peter Venema's Arithmetica, considered the first arithmetic text printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. In 1733 the politicians Rip Van Dam and Lewis Morris hired him to print The New-York Weekly Journal against the new governor, William Cosby, who had quarrelled with the council over his salary and removed Chief Justice Morris for ruling against him, replacing him with James DeLancey. The first issue reported Morris's victory over Cosby's candidate in an election at Eastchester.</p><p>Cosby condemned the paper's seditious reflections, and on 17 November 1734 the sheriff arrested Zenger. His first counsel, James Alexander, was disbarred for contempt. After more than eight months in prison Zenger was defended by Andrew Hamilton and William Smith, Sr. Rebuffed by DeLancey, Hamilton appealed directly to the jury, which returned after ten minutes on 5 August 1735 with a verdict of not guilty, an example of jury nullification. Their argument that truth is a defence had appeared in the paper in a Cato essay, the pen name of John Trenchard and Thomas Gordon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zenger died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 28 July 1746, and his wife carried on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> business. The case prepared the ground for press freedom rather than a legal precedent: later governors restricted the press until the Revolution, and only after People v. Croswell in 1804 did New York allow truth as a defence against libel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Peter Zenger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Zenger</b> trial of 1735 advanced press freedom, and the Albany Congress of 1754 met there.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-new-york">Province of New York</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Impflingen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.16,8.11&amp;year=1728" aria-label="Impflingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John Peter Zenger"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5452 5894h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5452 5894h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.16,8.11&amp;year=1728">What was here in 1728? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
